#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Ask the submitters of hidden Cell Browser datasets whether their paper has
been published yet, so datasets do not sit hidden forever.

Two steps, on purpose -- nothing reaches an author without a human looking
at it first:

  cbPingHidden scan     find datasets that are due for a reminder and write
                        one draft per dataset into the queue directory
  cbPingHidden send     show each queued draft and send it after you confirm

A dataset is due when it is hidden, still live on cells.ucsc.edu, has an
email address somewhere in its desc.conf, and has not been heard from in
MIN_AGE_DAYS -- counted from the last reminder we sent, or from its
submission date if we have never written.

The address usually sits in the "submitter" field, but plenty of desc.conf
files keep it somewhere else, so the scan works down a ladder: the contact
fields, the custom dict (an "Authors for correspondence" key and the like),
a mailto: link in the abstract, any other field, then loose text. A dataset
inside a collection falls back to the collection's own desc.conf. Where the
address came from is written into the draft and shown in the report, so an
unusual source can be eyeballed before anything goes out.

Delete a draft out of the queue directory to drop that dataset from the
batch. Edit a draft in place to change what gets sent.

Runs monthly from the otto crontab, where it only queues drafts and mails
the report to cells@ucsc.edu:

  0 8 1 * * umask 002; ~mspeir/cellBrowser/ucsc/cbPingHidden scan -q --mail-report cells@ucsc.edu
"""
import sys
import os
import re
import argparse
import subprocess
import email.utils
import textwrap
from datetime import date, datetime, timedelta
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor

import requests

